"""Build a small static report from measured output."""
from pathlib import Path
import csv,html
root=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
rows=list(csv.DictReader((root/'summary.csv').open()))
table=''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+html.escape(v)+'</td>' for v in [r['case'],r['solver'],'yes' if r['converged']=='1' else 'budget limit',r['iterations'],r['evaluations'],f"{float(r['residual']):.2e}",f"{float(r['median_us']):.1f}"] )+'</tr>' for r in rows)
figures=[('convergence','Watch convergence','Residual versus updates. The dotted line is the shared 1e-10 target. A plateau exposes persistent error.'),('work','Count the work','Map evaluations include numerical Jacobians and line searches. Two Newton updates can cost as many map calls as twenty relaxation updates.'),('runtime','Measure the latency','Median of 21 runs, with min–max whiskers. Hatched bars exhaust the update budget; their time is not time to successful convergence.'),('compatibility','Read the compatibility','Blue supports; red opposes; white is zero. Row: receiver; column: source. Each pair of coordinates belongs to one object’s two labels. Raw weights are multiplied by beta in the solver.'),('response-3d','See the response in three dimensions','Two views of the local softmax map: neighboring label strengths change the receiving object’s target. These are analytical response surfaces for the stated representative prior, not measured physical landscapes.'),('final-states','Check which fixed point','Strong agreement sends relaxation and Newton to different roots. In the conflicting ring only Newton meets the residual target. A small residual says the equation is satisfied; it does not select the meaning or stability of a preference state.')]
sections=''.join(f'<section><h2>{title}</h2><p>{desc}</p><a href="{name}.svg"><img src="{name}.svg" alt="{html.escape(desc)}" loading="lazy"></a><p class="small"><a href="{name}.png">PNG</a> · <a href="{name}.svg">Vector SVG</a></p></section>' for name,title,desc in figures)
page='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Compatibility, convergence & cost | Michael Emanuel Glover</title><style>
*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:#faf9f5;color:#203b40;font:17px/1.65 system-ui,sans-serif}main{max-width:1150px;margin:auto;padding:24px}a{color:#655096}header{padding:55px 0 35px}h1{font:clamp(40px,7vw,78px)/1.07 Georgia,serif;max-width:950px}h2{font:34px/1.2 Georgia,serif}section{padding:28px 0;border-top:1px solid #d5d9d5}img{width:100%;height:auto}.lede{font-size:23px;max-width:850px}.tag{color:#b05526;font-size:13px;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase}.cards{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:16px}.card{padding:20px;background:#eeede8;border-radius:10px}.small{font-size:14px}.scroll{overflow:auto}table{border-collapse:collapse;white-space:nowrap;font-size:14px;width:100%}td,th{text-align:left;padding:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd}pre{padding:20px;background:#203b40;color:white;overflow:auto}@media(max-width:650px){main{padding:18px}.cards{grid-template-columns:1fr}header{padding-top:25px}}
</style><main><nav><a href="../../">← Virtual Person & Cell</a> · <a href="README.md">Methods & interpretation</a></nav><header><p class="tag">Michael Emanuel Glover · numerical research notebook · 13 November 2026</p><h1>Compatibility.<br>Convergence. Cost.</h1><p class="lede">How fast does a field of preferences settle? Compare relaxation and Newton on the same C++ equation, with color, measured work and visible failure cases.</p><p>Michael’s question and Virtual Person framework; benchmark implementation and figures with Cypher. Five deterministic synthetic ring fields, three solvers, one residual target.</p></header><div class="cards"><div class="card"><b>Fewer iterations ≠ less time</b><p>At 32 objects, Newton takes 2 updates but 69 map calls. Full-step relaxation takes 20 updates and 21 calls, and is faster here.</p></div><div class="card"><b>Critical slowing matters</b><p>Near the contraction boundary, Newton reaches the target in 2 updates. Both relaxation settings miss it within 500.</p></div><div class="card"><b>The root matters too</b><p>Strong coupling sends the solvers to different fixed points. Newton can find roots that repel relaxation.</p></div></div>
<section><h2>One fair stopping test</h2><pre>T(p) = softmax(log prior + β C p)
F(p) = T(p) − p
success: max |F(p)| ≤ 10⁻¹⁰
relaxation: p ← p + η F(p), η ∈ {0.5, 1}
Newton: solve J_F Δ = −F, safeguard the step</pre><p>All methods start at the same prior and have a 500-update budget. Newton uses a finite-difference Jacobian, pivoted dense solve, feasibility safeguards and residual line search. Solvers reuse the existing <code>CompatibilityField::target</code> map. These are fixed-point experiments; no objective-minimization claim is imposed on signed compatibility.</p></section>'''+sections+'''<section><h2>Measured outcomes</h2><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Solver</th><th>Target met?</th><th>Updates</th><th>Map calls</th><th>Residual</th><th>Median µs</th></tr></thead><tbody>'''+table+'''</tbody></table></div><p class="small">Intel Core i7-1255U · GCC 11.4 · C++17 Release · one warmup, 21 timed repetitions, alternating solver order. Desktop timing includes allocations and trace capture. This is a latency baseline for edge-computing research; device power and energy remain unmeasured.</p></section><section><h2>Keep testing, keep the evidence</h2><p>Run the same fixtures after changes; compare residuals, map calls, wall time and selected roots. Gentle and near-critical fields have a global contraction bound β&lt;1. Stronger cases expose limits of those guarantees. Local derivative diagnostics confirm that Newton’s strong-agreement and conflicting roots are unstable under both tested relaxation updates. Five examples do not establish performance across arbitrary fields.</p><p><a href="README.md">Full methods & next experiments</a> · <a href="environment.txt">Build environment</a> · <a href="summary.csv">Summary CSV</a> · <a href="traces.csv">Every residual</a> · <a href="states.csv">Terminal states</a> · <a href="compatibility.csv">Compatibility matrices</a> · <a href="stability.csv">Local stability</a></p><p><a href="../../include/fixed_point_comparison.hpp">Read the solver classes</a> · <a href="../../src/convergence_benchmark.cpp">Read the C++ experiment</a> · <a href="../../tests/convergence_tests.cpp">Checks</a> · <a href="run.sh">Repeat this experiment</a> · <a href="../../virtual-cell-source.zip">Existing project source ZIP</a></p><p class="small">All 17 native project test suites passed, including shared-map arithmetic, agreement in a contractive field, normalization, zero-prior anchoring, residual stopping, pivoting and singular-solve checks. No measured run used the Newton fallback. Finite-difference and dense-solve costs belong to this implementation; analytic or sparse Newton may change the comparison.</p></section><footer>Michael Emanuel Glover · Virtual Cell / Virtual Person · Glover Machine Works</footer></main></html>'''
